      "math_academy_id": "q-65848",
      "knowledge_point_id": "fc8166bb-3a9d-5808-af53-a18ca99920f8",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the General Solution to an Equation Containing a Transformed Sine Function",
      "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi ,\\,x=b\\pi +2n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Which of the following could be the value of $b?$",
      "worked_solution": "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $b=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
