      "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{60}^{∘})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=b+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x={195}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $b$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{180}^{∘}).$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$",
      "worked_solution": "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{60}^{∘} & ={210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\, & \\, & 2x-{60}^{∘} & ={330}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={210}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={330}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 2x & ={270}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={390}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={135}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={195}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $b={135}^{∘}.$",
