      "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+{30}^{∘})=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a+n\\cdot {45}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{45}^{∘}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$",
      "worked_solution": "The given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+{30}^{∘})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+{30}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={90}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={15}^{∘}.$",
