      "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{30}^{∘})=1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a+n\\cdot {90}^{∘},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value $a$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{90}^{∘}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$",
      "worked_solution": "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{30}^{∘} & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={45}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={75}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={37.5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=37.5.$",
