      "problem": "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +2n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[0,2).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=\\frac{1}{6}.$",
